<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albi is a city on the Tarn in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, capital of the Tarn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. Its name was applied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a>, called Albigensians, and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> launched against them in 1209. Its fortress-like brick cathedral of Sainte-Cécile, begun in 1282, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' palace, now the Toulouse-Lautrec Museum, form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albi.jpg" alt="Albi" width="320" height="152" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Albi)_North_views_of_the_Ste_C%C3%A9cile_Cathedral_and_the_Old_Bridge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
